data.content:
Here are the three breaking changes and their fixes:

## 1. `Buffer.cs` — `[InlineArray]` on `record struct` banned in C# 13

C# 13 prohibits `[InlineArray]` on `record struct` types (they synthesize methods incompatible with inline arrays). Fix: use a plain `struct`.

```csharp
[System.Runtime.CompilerServices.InlineArray(10)]
struct Buffer
{
    private int _element0;
}
```

## 2. `Generator.cs` — `stackalloc` in local function inside iterator

C# 13 relaxed unsafe restrictions in iterators/async methods, which means the local function is now analyzed *as part of* the iterator. `stackalloc` is still prohibited in iterator methods. Fix: extract to a regular static method.

```csharp
unsafe class DataGenerator
{
    IEnumerable<int> Generate()
    {
        yield return 1;
        Process();
    }

    static void Process()
    {
        int* p = stackalloc int[10];
        // ... work with pointer
    }
}
```

## 3. `Resolver.cs` — `[]` collection expression now ambiguous

C# 13's improved overload resolution means `[]` is now convertible to both `ReadOnlySpan<int>` and `Span<object>`, making the call ambiguous. Fix: disambiguate with an explicit type.

```csharp
class Resolver
{
    static void Process(ReadOnlySpan<int> ros) {}
    static void Process(Span<object> s) {}

    static void Main()
    {
        Process(ReadOnlySpan<int>.Empty);  // explicit target type
    }
}
```

**Summary:** These are all compile-time breaking changes in C# 13 — no runtime surprises. The pattern is that C# 13 tightened rules around inline arrays and unsafe-in-iterators, while broadening collection expression conversions (which surfaces new ambiguities).
